Those two phrases fit together as complementary images.

* “A thousand fallen stones” evokes gradual accumulation. No single stone changes the landscape much, but enough of them eventually create a path, a wall, or an arch. The emphasis is on persistence and incremental construction rather than a single breakthrough.
* “The golden arch” evokes emergence: once enough stones are placed in the right relationship, a new structure appears that none of the individual stones possesses on its own. An arch is interesting because each stone supports the others. The whole becomes stable through its arrangement.

Taken together, the metaphor suggests something like:

Many small contributions, organized well, eventually create a structure that can support weight far beyond any individual piece.

That aligns with a recurring theme in your project: favoring many reusable, interconnected ideas over one massive monolith. The power comes not only from the number of pieces, but from how they reinforce one another.

As a general operator, you could express it as:

Arch Principle: Accumulate small, durable units. Arrange them so each supports the others. When the network reaches the right configuration, new capabilities emerge that were unavailable to any isolated component.

It’s a nice complement to your spider/web metaphor. The web emphasizes sensing and adaptation; the arch emphasizes stability through relationships. One is dynamic, the other structural, but both rely on the same underlying idea: the organization of the parts matters more than the parts alone.